<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simplon Pass</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Simplon Pass is an Alpine pass at about 2,005 metres between Brig in the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domodossola">Domodossola</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It was a medieval trade route, controlled in the 17th century by the Brig merchant Kaspar Stockalper. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had a carriage road built over it between 1801 and 1805, and the Simplon railway tunnel beneath the area opened in 1906.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simplon-pass.jpg" alt="Simplon Pass" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zacharie Grossen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sempione.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Simplon Pass is a high mountain pass between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennine-alps">Pennine Alps</a> and the Lepontine Alps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. It connects Brig in the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domodossola">Domodossola</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The pass itself and the villages on each side of it, such as Gondo, lie in Switzerland. Its lowest point, which is also the lowest point of the watershed between the basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and the Po in Switzerland, lies in marshland about 500 metres west of the settlement of Simplon Pass. Peaks that can be climbed from the pass include the Wasenhorn, the Hubschhorn, the Breithorn and Monte Leone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A passage through the mountains had been used locally for several centuries, but the pass acquired international significance during the Napoleonic occupation. Between 1801 and 1805 the engineer Nicolas Céard built the Simplon Road at the direction of the emperor, so that artillery could be moved between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. From then on the pass could be used by road vehicles: first post carriages, replaced early in the twentieth century by post buses. The former département of Simplon, the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>, was named after the pass.</p><p>The cantonal authorities made a plan in 1950 to keep the road open all year instead of closing it between October and late April, as was usual at this altitude. It called for avalanche shelters and wider tunnels for full-size coaches. In October 1970 journalists were told that the work had been finished on 37 of the 42.5 kilometres between Brig and the Italian frontier at Gondo. The Simplon Tunnel, 20 kilometres long, opened in 1906, and the Orient Express used the Simplon route intermittently during the twentieth century on its way between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The road remains one of the few major Alpine passes kept open throughout the year. The Hospice du Simplon at the top is owned by the Congregation of Canons Regular at Grand-Saint-Bernard. A granite eagle about nine metres high, designed by the architect Erwin Friedrich Baumann and inaugurated in September 1944, commemorates the 11th Alpine Brigade of the Swiss Army; a memorial of 2005 marks the 200th anniversary of the Napoleon Road.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simplon Pass” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its economy rested on Alpine farming and trade over the <b>Simplon</b> pass, dominated by families such as the Stockalper of Brig.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-the-seven-tenths">Republic of the Seven Tenths</a></li><li><blockquote>Napoleon wanted control of the <b>Simplon</b> pass between France and Italy.</blockquote><a href="/state/rhodanic-republic">Rhodanic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
